{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna served as regent until he came of age. In 1164 he was captured by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and held prisoner for nearly ten years until a ransom was paid in 1174. Upon his release he returned to Tripoli and resumed control of the county.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early life and accession**\nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. His father was assassinated in 1152 by a group of Hashashin leaving Raymond III as his heir. Since he was still a minor his mother Hodierna acted as regent. She ruled effectively until Raymond III reached adulthood around 1155. His early reign was marked by ongoing conflicts with the Muslim powers in the region especially Nur ad-Din.\n\n**Conflict and captivity**\nRaymond III faced constant pressure from Nur ad-Din who sought to expand his influence into the County of Tripoli. In 1164 Raymond III joined a coalition of Crusader states to confront Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim. The Crusaders were decisively defeated and Raymond III was taken captive. He spent nearly a decade in prison until his release in 1174 following a heavy ransom paid by Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus and King Amalric of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Rule and political struggles**\nAfter his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and resumed his rule. He worked to stabilize the county which had been weakened during his absence. He formed alliances with other Crusader states and sought to strengthen Tripoli’s defenses. His primary concern was defending against Muslim advances particularly from Saladin who was rising as a major power in the region. Raymond III also had to navigate the complex politics between the Crusader states and the Byzantine Empire which had supported his ransom.\n\n**Later years and death**\nIn the 1180s Raymond III became increasingly involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a key supporter of Guy of Lusignan though he later switched his allegiance to Conrad of Montferrat. In 1187 he played a crucial role in the Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army was crushed by Saladin. Raymond III died shortly after on 23 August 1187 either from illness or injuries sustained in battle. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli which would soon face further challenges under Muslim expansion.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Legacy**\nRaymond III is remembered as a steadfast ruler who defended Tripoli during a tumultuous period. His long captivity and eventual return demonstrated his resilience and commitment to his county. Though his reign ended in tragedy with the loss of Jerusalem and the broader collapse of Crusader states his efforts helped prolong the existence of the County of Tripoli. Historians often note his diplomatic skills and ability to maintain alliances despite the shifting power dynamics of the time. His legacy is one of perseverance in the face of overwhelming odds.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.3243790864944458}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.3638601303100586}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.09140915912576e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.012362048029899597}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -8.201262971851975e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.768360213347478e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.3183533251285553}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.14469268918037415}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.9430925021879375e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00021371940965764225}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.28973811864852905}